# Self-similarity

**Self-similarity** is the property of a structure that resembles itself at
other scales: a part looks like the whole, or like a transformed copy of it.
It is the signature of a [[fractal]]. In this vault its role is that of the
first thing a [[strange-loop]] gets mistaken for, and the answer is short:
self-similarity is not [[self-reference]].

## Description

The question that prompted this card was whether a strange loop differs from a
fractal or merely resembles one. Both invite the phrase "it contains itself",
and both are usually produced by repeating a rule. The resemblance stops
there.

| Property | Fractal | Strange loop |
|---|---|---|
| Signature | Repetition related by scale | Self-reference through a crossing of levels |
| Usual mechanism | Iteration, recursive geometry | Encoding, interpretation, reflective closure |
| Example | The Mandelbrot set | The Gödel sentence, Escher's hands |
| Is recursion enough? | Not by itself | No |

The Mandelbrot set comes from iterating

$$z_{n+1}=z_n^2+c.$$

The rule is applied to its own output again and again, and the picture that
results repeats its shapes at every magnification. Nothing in it says
anything about the rule. The set does not encode its own definition, and no
part of it stands for the whole. It *looks like* itself; it does not *refer
to* itself.

The two movements can be drawn side by side.

```mermaid
flowchart LR
 subgraph Similarity
 A[Pattern] --> B[Smaller related pattern] --> C[Another scale]
 end
 subgraph Reflection
 D[Formal object] --> E[Encoded description] --> F[Statement about the object] --> D
 end
```

Similarity runs in one direction, down the scales, and never comes back.
Reflection passes through a description and returns to the thing described.
The first stays on one level of description throughout: every stage is a
shape. The second changes level twice, from object to code and from code to a
statement about the object.

The two properties are independent. A structure can have both, since nothing
stops a self-referential system from also repeating across scales, but
neither implies the other.

For this reason fractals appear on every list of anti-examples drawn up in the
discussion, beside the factorial function and ordinary cyclic feedback. In the
[[taxonomy-of-self-reference]] they fall into the first class, recursive
generation, where a rule repeats on its own results and no representation is
involved.

## Relations

- It is not [[self-reference]] {relation: distinguished-from}: resembling oneself is not referring to oneself.
- It is not a [[strange-loop]] {relation: distinguished-from}: it descends through scales without crossing a level of description.
- It is the defining property of a [[fractal]] {relation: part-of}.
- It is usually produced by [[recursion]] {relation: uses} or by iterating a rule.
- It lacks [[level-crossing]] {relation: contrasts}: every stage is the same kind of object as the last.
- It belongs to the class of recursive generation in the [[taxonomy-of-self-reference]] {relation: instance-of}.
- Its reflective counterpart in the comparison is the [[godel-sentence]] {relation: contrasts}.
- It bears on the question [[strange-loop-meaning]] {relation: answers}, as a boundary case.

## Neighbouring concepts

- **[[self-reference]].** A statement, program or system that refers to or
  represents itself. Self-similarity is the weakest thing the word is used
  for, and strictly it does not qualify.
- **[[strange-loop]].** A return to the starting level after crossing levels
  of description. A fractal has the repetition and none of the crossing.
- **[[recursion]].** Definition through a smaller instance of the same thing.
  It is the mechanism behind most self-similar structures and, like them, an
  anti-example.
- **[[level-crossing]].** The step from an object to its description or back.
  Its absence is what the comparison table above records.

